(b) (b) You must visually examine the supplier's invoice, guarantee, or certification in a shipment you receive to ensure the components are consistent with your purchase order;
(c) (c) You must quarantine components before you use them in the manufacture of a dietary supplement until:
(c)(1) (1) You collect representative samples of each unique lot of components (and, for components that you receive, of each unique shipment, and of each unique lot within each unique shipment);
(c)(2) (2) Quality control personnel review and approve the results of any tests or examinations conducted on components; and
(c)(3) (3) Quality control personnel approve the components for use in the manufacture of a dietary supplement, including approval of any treatment (including in-process adjustments) of components to make them suitable for use in the manufacture of a dietary supplement, and releases them from quarantine.
(d)(1) (d)(1) You must identify each unique lot within each unique shipment of components that you receive and any lot of components that you produce in a manner that allows you to trace the lot to the supplier, the date received, the name of the component, the status of the component (e.g., quarantined, approved, or rejected); and to the dietary supplement that you manufactured and distributed.
(d)(2) (2) You must use this unique identifier whenever you record the disposition of each unique lot within each unique shipment of components that you receive and any lot of components that you produce.
(e) (e) You must hold components under conditions that will protect against contamination and deterioration, and avoid mixups.

(a) (a) The name of the dietary supplement to be manufactured and the strength, concentration, weight, or measure of each dietary ingredient for each batch size;
(b) (b) A complete list of components to be used;
(c) (c) An accurate statement of the weight or measure of each component to be used;
(d) (d) The identity and weight or measure of each dietary ingredient that will be declared on the Supplement Facts label and the identity of each ingredient that will be declared on the ingredients list of the dietary supplement;
(e) (e) A statement of any intentional overage amount of a dietary ingredient;
(f) (f) A statement of theoretical yield of a manufactured dietary supplement expected at each point, step, or stage of the manufacturing process where control is needed to ensure the quality of the dietary supplement, and the expected yield when you finish manufacturing the dietary supplement, including the maximum and minimum percentages of theoretical yield beyond which a deviation investigation of a batch is necessary and material review is conducted and disposition decision is made;
(g) (g) A description of packaging and a representative label, or a cross-reference to the physical location of the actual or representative label;
(h) (h) Written instructions, including the following:
(h)(1) (1) Specifications for each point, step, or stage in the manufacturing process where control is necessary to ensure the quality of the dietary supplement and that the dietary supplement is packaged and labeled as specified in the master manufacturing record;
(h)(2) (2) Procedures for sampling and a cross-reference to procedures for tests or examinations;
(h)(3) (3) Specific actions necessary to perform and verify points, steps, or stages in the manufacturing process where control is necessary to ensure the quality of the dietary supplement and that the dietary supplement is packaged and labeled as specified in the master manufacturing record.
(i) (i) Such specific actions must include verifying the weight or measure of any component and verifying the addition of any component; and
(i)(ii) (ii) For manual operations, such specific actions must include:
(i)(ii)(A) (A) One person weighing or measuring a component and another person verifying the weight or measure; and
(i)(ii)(B) (B) One person adding the component and another person verifying the addition.
(i)(ii)(B)(4) (4) Special notations and precautions to be followed; and
(i)(ii)(B)(5) (5) Corrective action plans for use when a specification is not met.
(section) The master manufacturing record must include:

(a) (a) You must prepare a batch production record every time you manufacture a batch of a dietary supplement;
(b) (b) Your batch production record must include complete information relating to the production and control of each batch;
(c) (c) Your batch production record must accurately follow the appropriate master manufacturing record and you must perform each step in the production of the batch; and
(d) (d) You must make and keep batch production records in accordance with subpart P of this part.

(a) (a) The batch, lot, or control number:
(a)(1) (1) Of the finished batch of dietary supplement; and
(a)(2) (2) That you assign in accordance with § 111.415(f) for the following:
(a)(2)(i) (i) Each lot of packaged and labeled dietary supplement from the finished batch of dietary supplement;
(a)(2)(ii) (ii) Each lot of dietary supplement, from the finished batch of dietary supplement, that you distribute to another person for packaging or labeling;
(b) (b) The identity of equipment and processing lines used in producing the batch;
(c) (c) The date and time of the maintenance, cleaning, and sanitizing of the equipment and processing lines used in producing the batch, or a cross-reference to records, such as individual equipment logs, where this information is retained;
(d) (d) The unique identifier that you assigned to each component (or, when applicable, to a product that you receive from a supplier for packaging or labeling as a dietary supplement), packaging, and label used;
(e) (e) The identity and weight or measure of each component used;
(f) (f) A statement of the actual yield and a statement of the percentage of theoretical yield at appropriate phases of processing;
(g) (g) The actual results obtained during any monitoring operation;
(h) (h) The results of any testing or examination performed during the batch production, or a cross-reference to such results;
(i) (i) Documentation that the finished dietary supplement meets specifications established in accordance with § 111.70(e) and (g);
(j) (j) Documentation, at the time of performance, of the manufacture of the batch, including:
(j)(1) (1) The date on which each step of the master manufacturing record was performed; and
(j)(2) (2) The initials of the persons performing each step, including:
(j)(2)(i) (i) The initials of the person responsible for weighing or measuring each component used in the batch;
(j)(2)(ii) (ii) The initials of the person responsible for verifying the weight or measure of each component used in the batch;
(j)(2)(iii) (iii) The initials of the person responsible for adding the component to the batch; and
(j)(2)(iv) (iv) The initials of the person responsible for verifying the addition of components to the batch;
(k) (k) Documentation, at the time of performance, of packaging and labeling operations, including:
(k)(1) (1) The unique identifier that you assigned to packaging and labels used, the quantity of the packaging and labels used, and, when label reconciliation is required, reconciliation of any discrepancies between issuance and use of labels;
(k)(2) (2) An actual or representative label, or a cross-reference to the physical location of the actual or representative label specified in the master manufacturing record; and
(k)(3) (3) The results of any tests or examinations conducted on packaged and labeled dietary supplements (including repackaged or relabeled dietary supplements), or a cross-reference to the physical location of such results;
(l) (l) Documentation at the time of performance that quality control personnel:
(l)(1) (1) Reviewed the batch production record, including:
(l)(1)(i) (i) Review of any monitoring operation required under subpart E of this part; and
(l)(1)(ii) (ii) Review of the results of any tests and examinations, including tests and examinations conducted on components, in-process materials, finished batches of dietary supplements, and packaged and labeled dietary supplements;
(l)(2) (2) Approved or rejected any reprocessing or repackaging; and
(l)(3) (3) Approved and released, or rejected, the batch for distribution, including any reprocessed batch; and
(l)(4) (4) Approved and released, or rejected, the packaged and labeled dietary supplement, including any repackaged or relabeled dietary supplement.
(m) (m) Documentation at the time of performance of any required material review and disposition decision.
(n) (n) Documentation at the time of performance of any reprocessing.
(section) The batch production record must include the following:

(a) (a) Performing manufacturing operations under conditions and controls that protect against the potential for growth of microorganisms and the potential for contamination;
(b) (b) Washing or cleaning components that contain soil or other contaminants;
(c) (c) Using water that, at a minimum, complies with the applicable Federal, State, and local requirements and does not contaminate the dietary supplement when the water may become a component of the finished batch of dietary supplement;
(d) (d) Performing chemical, microbiological, or other testing, as necessary to prevent the use of contaminated components;
(e) (e) Sterilizing, pasteurizing, freezing, refrigerating, controlling hydrogen-ion concentration (pH), controlling humidity, controlling water activity (aw), or using any other effective means to remove, destroy, or prevent the growth of microorganisms and prevent decomposition;
(f) (f) Holding components and dietary supplements that can support the rapid growth of microorganisms of public health significance in a manner that prevents the components and dietary supplements from becoming adulterated;
(g) (g) Identifying and holding any components or dietary supplements, for which a material review and disposition decision is required, in a manner that protects components or dietary supplements that are not under a material review against contamination and mixups with those that are under a material review;
(h) (h) Performing mechanical manufacturing steps (such as cutting, sorting, inspecting, shredding, drying, grinding, blending, and sifting) by any effective means to protect the dietary supplements against contamination, by, for example:
(h)(1) (1) Cleaning and sanitizing contact surfaces;
(h)(2) (2) Using temperature controls; and
(h)(3) (3) Using time controls.
(i) (i) Using effective measures to protect against the inclusion of metal or other foreign material in components or dietary supplements, by, for example:
(i)(1) (1) Filters or strainers,
(i)(2) (2) Traps,
(i)(3) (3) Magnets, or
(i)(4) (4) Electronic metal detectors.
(j) (j) Segregating and identifying all containers for a specific batch of dietary supplements to identify their contents and, when necessary, the phase of manufacturing; and
(k) (k) Identifying all processing lines and major equipment used during manufacturing to indicate their contents, including the name of the dietary supplement and the specific batch or lot number and, when necessary, the phase of manufacturing.
(section) You must take all the necessary precautions during the manufacture of a dietary supplement to prevent contamination of components or dietary supplements. These precautions include:
